import{H as e,at as t,it as n,vt as r}from"./chunks/framework.dmHZ7NHG.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"The quantum computer","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"params":{"slug":"quantum","address":"15b6960e-063d-841b-998a-5ae100ae17cf","receipt":"24817c7a-273d-8ac9-a1e6-3604d094468c"},"relativePath":"docs/publications/quantum.md","filePath":"docs/publications/[slug].md"}`),a={name:`docs/publications/quantum.md`};function o(e,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),n(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="the-quantum-computer" tabindex="-1">The quantum computer <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-quantum-computer" aria-label="Permalink to “The quantum computer”">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>the exact facts the classical state-vector simulator computes — Born rule, no-signaling, GHZ, gate truth-tables, phase algebra; simulation, not hardware</p></blockquote><p>These 42 facts hold by decision. Each was proven in Lean 4 — no Mathlib, checked sorry-free — and every sentence below links the proof that seals it. This note claims nothing beyond what its theorems settle: not that the domain is complete, only that the prose says nothing the proofs do not. It was written by reading the sealed ledger; read the proofs, not the prose.</p><h2 id="the-facts-each-backed-by-its-proof" tabindex="-1">The facts, each backed by its proof <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-facts-each-backed-by-its-proof" aria-label="Permalink to “The facts, each backed by its proof”">​</a></h2><ul><li>the Bell state (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2 — the Born-rule weights |amp|² are [1,0,0,1]: only |00⟩ and |11⟩ are ever observed, |01⟩ and |10⟩ never (probability 0) — <a href="/theorem/bell_born_weights">proof</a>.</li><li>Bell normalization: Σ|amp|² = 1+0+0+1 = 2 = 2¹ (scale 1) — the weights are an exact probability distribution, no floating point — <a href="/theorem/bell_normalized">proof</a>.</li><li>perfect correlation: the two qubits always agree — the outcomes carrying weight are exactly the basis states {00, 11} (indices where bit q0 equals bit q1) — <a href="/theorem/bell_perfect_correlation">proof</a>.</li><li>no-signaling (the paradox, computed): the two marginals of q0 are equal — weight(q0=0)=1²+0² = 0²+1²=weight(q0=1) — so measuring q1 sends NOTHING to q0 (no-communication) — <a href="/theorem/bell_no_signaling">proof</a>.</li><li>superposition H|0⟩ = |+⟩ — the Born weights are [1,1] over √2, so P(0)=P(1)=1/2: before measurement both, after, one — <a href="/theorem/superposition_h0">proof</a>.</li><li>GHZ(3) = (|000⟩+|111⟩)/√2 — of the 2³ = 8 basis outcomes exactly two carry weight (the all-0 and all-1 corners); three-party entanglement — <a href="/theorem/ghz3_two_outcomes">proof</a>.</li><li>GHZ(3) normalization: Σ|amp|² = 1²+1² = 2 = 2¹ — an exact distribution over the two correlated corners — <a href="/theorem/ghz3_normalized">proof</a>.</li><li>CNOT(q0→q1) flips q1 iff q0 is set — the basis permutation i ↦ i ⊕ 2·(q0) = [0,3,2,1] on two qubits — <a href="/theorem/cnot_truth_table">proof</a>.</li><li>CNOT is its own inverse: applying it twice returns every basis state — a reversible (unitary) permutation — <a href="/theorem/cnot_involution">proof</a>.</li><li>Toffoli (CCX) flips q2 iff q0 ∧ q1 — the reversible classical AND: i ↦ i ⊕ 4·(q0·q1) = [0,1,2,7,4,5,6,3] on three qubits — <a href="/theorem/toffoli_truth_table">proof</a>.</li><li>SWAP exchanges q0 and q1 — the basis permutation i ↦ 2·q0 + q1 = [0,2,1,3] on two qubits — <a href="/theorem/swap_truth_table">proof</a>.</li><li>S·S = Z: two phase gates compose to the Z phase-flip (i² = −1), verified exactly on sample Gaussian-integer amplitudes S(re,im)=(−im,re) — <a href="/theorem/s_squared_is_z">proof</a>.</li><li>Z² = I: the phase flip is its own inverse — negating an amplitude twice returns it, on sample Gaussian-integer amplitudes — <a href="/theorem/z_involution">proof</a>.</li><li>S·S† = I: the phase gate and its adjoint invert — S(re,im)=(−im,re) then S†(re,im)=(im,−re) returns the amplitude — <a href="/theorem/s_dagger_inverse">proof</a>.</li><li>X² = I: the bit-flip is its own inverse — flip q0 twice (i ⊕ 1 ⊕ 1) returns every basis state; X is an involution — <a href="/theorem/pauli_x_involution">proof</a>.</li><li>SWAP² = I: exchanging q0 and q1 twice returns every basis state — SWAP is an involution — <a href="/theorem/swap_involution">proof</a>.</li><li>Toffoli² = I: the reversible AND is its own inverse — applying CCX twice returns every basis state; Toffoli is an involution — <a href="/theorem/toffoli_involution">proof</a>.</li><li>CZ² = I: the |11⟩ phase-flip squared is the identity — the sign (1 − 2·q0·q1) ∈ {+1,−1} squares to +1; CZ is an involution — <a href="/theorem/cz_involution">proof</a>.</li><li>H² = I on |0⟩: two Hadamards give amplitudes [2,0] at scale 2, which canonicalize (÷2, dropping scale by 2) to |0⟩ = [1,0]; H is an involution — <a href="/theorem/h_involution_on_zero">proof</a>.</li><li>S⁴ = I but S² = Z ≠ I: the phase gate has ORDER 4 (i⁴=1), so S is NOT an involution — the honest exception; multiplying an amplitude by i four times returns it — <a href="/theorem/s_fourth_is_identity">proof</a>.</li><li>Deutsch–Jozsa interference: a BALANCED boolean sends equal +1/−1 phases, which cancel to 0 — the query amplitude vanishes. The honest heart of the algorithm, as the simulator computes it (classical linear algebra, no advantage) — <a href="/theorem/dj_balanced_cancels">proof</a>.</li><li>Deutsch–Jozsa: a CONSTANT boolean sends one phase, so all four reinforce to ±4 — the opposite of the balanced cancellation. Constant vs balanced IS exactly this interference sum — <a href="/theorem/dj_constant_reinforces">proof</a>.</li><li>The entanglement witness: a two-qubit state (a,b,c,d) factorizes into a product iff a·d − b·c = 0. Bell (1,0,0,1) gives 1 ≠ 0 (ENTANGLED); |00⟩ (1,0,0,0) and |+0⟩ (1,1,0,0) give 0 (separable) — entanglement is the nonzero determinant, computed exactly — <a href="/theorem/entanglement_determinant">proof</a>.</li><li>Pauli X and Z ANTICOMMUTE (XZ = −ZX): X flips the bit, Z stamps (−1)^bit, and (−1)^b = −(−1)^(1−b) on both bits — the sign the simulator carries; the nonabelian core of the gate algebra — <a href="/theorem/pauli_x_z_anticommute">proof</a>.</li><li>The W state (|001⟩+|010⟩+|100⟩)/√3 — exactly THREE of the 2³ corners carry weight (vs GHZ’s two): a distinct entanglement class, robust to one-party loss. The simulator’s amplitude vector, counted — <a href="/theorem/w_state_three_outcomes">proof</a>.</li><li>W-state normalization: Σ|amp|² = 1+1+1 = 3 over √3 — an exact distribution over the three single-excitation corners — <a href="/theorem/w_state_normalized">proof</a>.</li><li>The four Bell states form a complete ORTHOGONAL basis: ⟨Φ⁺|Φ⁻⟩ = 0 and ⟨Ψ⁺|Ψ⁻⟩ = 0 (over √2 integer vectors), while ⟨Φ⁺|Φ⁺⟩ = 2 — the entangled-basis measurement, as exact integer inner products — <a href="/theorem/bell_basis_orthogonal">proof</a>.</li><li>n qubits span 2ⁿ amplitudes: [1,2,3,4,5] qubits give [2,4,8,16,32] — the state vector grows EXPONENTIALLY, which is exactly why simulating it classically is costly. HONEST SCOPE: this counts the simulation cost, it is NOT a speedup or a quantum advantage. — <a href="/theorem/n_qubit_dimension">proof</a>.</li><li>Combining systems MULTIPLIES their dimensions (the tensor product): two qubits span 2·2 = 4 amplitudes, three span 2·2·2 = 8. Independent subsystems compose by product, the source of the exponential. — <a href="/theorem/tensor_dimension_multiplies">proof</a>.</li><li>The single-qubit Pauli group is {I, X, Y, Z} × {±1, ±i} — 4 operators times 4 phases = 16 elements. The finite group the whole gate algebra is built over, counted. — <a href="/theorem/pauli_group_order_16">proof</a>.</li><li>The single-qubit Clifford group (the gates that permute the Paulis) has order 24 = 6 · 4 — six signed axes for X&#39;s image, four for the phase. Finite: the Cliffords are classically simulable (Gottesman–Knill), the honest reason they are NOT the source of advantage. — <a href="/theorem/clifford_group_order_24">proof</a>.</li><li>The phase gates form an order ladder: T has order 8, S = T² has order 4, Z = S² has order 2 — each the square of the next (8 = 2·4, 4 = 2·2) — and T⁸ = I is a full 2π turn (8 mod 8 = 0). Squaring a phase gate halves its order. — <a href="/theorem/phase_gate_order_ladder">proof</a>.</li><li>The CHSH game: quantum correlations exceed every local hidden variable — the Tsirelson value 2√2 beats the classical bound 2. Sealed as the SQUARED comparison (2√2 is irrational): 2² = 4 &lt; 8 = 2³. HONEST SCOPE: the simulator computes the correlation exactly; the squared bound is what decides — and no signal crosses (nothing FTL). — <a href="/theorem/chsh_beats_classical">proof</a>.</li><li>The dimension obstruction behind no-cloning: a cloner of an n-qubit state would need to write into (2ⁿ)² dimensions from 2ⁿ, but a unitary preserves dimension — 2² = 4 &lt; 16 = (2²)². HONEST SCOPE: this is the arithmetic SHADOW of the no-cloning theorem (a linearity fact), not a proof of it. — <a href="/theorem/no_cloning_dimension">proof</a>.</li><li>The Hadamard swaps the X and Z bases: HXH = Z, verified on integer amplitudes up to the √2² = 2 scale — HXH[a,b] = [2a, −2b] = 2·Z[a,b], on sample amplitudes. The conjugation that turns a bit-flip into a phase-flip, the heart of the Clifford structure. — <a href="/theorem/hadamard_conjugates_x_to_z">proof</a>.</li><li>The Bell state |Φ⁺⟩ = [1,0,0,1] is a +1 eigenstate of XX: flipping both bits reverses the amplitude vector (00↔11, 01↔10), and [1,0,0,1] is its own reverse — a stabiliser. The entanglement, read as a fixed point. — <a href="/theorem/bell_stabilized_by_xx">proof</a>.</li><li>The Bell state is a +1 eigenstate of ZZ too: its supported corners {00, 11} both have EVEN bit-parity ((0+0) and (1+1) are 0 mod 2), so ZZ stamps +1 on each. Two stabilisers XX and ZZ pin the state — the stabiliser formalism, in miniature. — <a href="/theorem/bell_zz_even_parity">proof</a>.</li><li>GHZ(3) = [1,0,0,0,0,0,0,1] is a +1 eigenstate of XXX: flipping all three bits reverses the 8-amplitude vector (000↔111), and the GHZ vector is its own reverse. The three-party entanglement, stabilised. — <a href="/theorem/ghz_stabilized_by_xxx">proof</a>.</li><li>Superdense coding: one qubit carries 2 classical bits — Alice&#39;s four local operations map |Φ⁺⟩ to the four orthogonal Bell states, 2² = 4 distinguishable messages, and 2 &gt; 1. HONEST SCOPE: this REQUIRES a pre-shared EPR pair; it is not bandwidth from nothing, and nothing signals faster than light. — <a href="/theorem/superdense_two_bits">proof</a>.</li><li>Teleportation sends one qubit with 2 classical bits and one EPR pair: Bob applies one of the four Pauli corrections {I, X, Z, XZ} indexed by the 2 measured bits (2+2 = 4 = the four corrections). HONEST SCOPE: the classical channel is ESSENTIAL — without the 2 bits nothing arrives, so no faster-than-light transfer. — <a href="/theorem/teleportation_four_corrections">proof</a>.</li><li>The computer&#39;s memory receipt is ORDER-INVARIANT — every ordering of the members folds to the SAME root under the axiom-free XOR (lxor), the same operation the gate permutations use, so the store recomputes for any observer in any order (the 3-member fold equals all six permutations). HONEST SCOPE: the classical content-address receipt the state folds to, integrity — not a quantum memory. — <a href="/theorem/store_fold_order_invariant">proof</a>.</li><li>The memory receipt refuses DRIFT — a changed member MOVES the fold: [a,b,c] folds to [a2,b,c]&#39;s value iff a = a2, so any edit to a memory is visible (tamper-evident), the change-sensitivity of the XOR fold. HONEST SCOPE: integrity of the content-address, not a quantum property. — <a href="/theorem/store_fold_change_moves_receipt">proof</a>.</li></ul><h2 id="provenance" tabindex="-1">Provenance <a class="header-anchor" href="#provenance" aria-label="Permalink to “Provenance”">​</a></h2><p>These 42 proofs fold, order-invariant, to the receipt <code>24817c7a-273d-8ac9-a1e6-3604d094468c</code> — recompute it from the same ledger and it returns. The note itself content-addresses to a uuid, so any edit is visible. Writing descends from reading: this note holds only what the proofs it links already sealed, and it was audited by uuidna&#39;s own honesty gate before it was published — a sentence that overreached a proof would have been refused, not shipped.</p><p>Source · <a href="https://github.com/uuidna/uuidna/blob/main/lean/Quantum.lean" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">lean/Quantum.lean</a> · <a href="/theorems">all theorems</a></p><hr><p><strong>Audited before published</strong> · this note passed uuidna&#39;s honesty gate at build time — every claim above links the proof that seals it. Content-address <code>15b6960e</code> · proofs fold to receipt <code>24817c7a</code> (the handles; the full uuids are in this page&#39;s meta, recomputable) · 42 sealed theorems.</p><p><a href="/publications">All publications</a> · <a href="/theorems">All theorems</a> · <a href="/trials">How auditing works</a></p>`,11)]])}var s=e(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};